uman behavior humans are pretty clever  and any regulation you put in they're  going to think of a way to get around it  take the fishing season government  regulators try to  her fish populations by limiting the  fishing season maybe do a couple of  months or a couple of weeks the problem  is it doesn't limit the catch it limits  the time so what I do is I go buy a  bigger faster boat and I can double my  catch in that period you see it on the  TV show The Deadliest Catch where  fishermen use everything from faster  boats to better navigation equipment to  catch as much crab as quickly as  possible and almost all scientists now  agree that the you know widespread  collapse of fisheries around the world  is due to mismanagement why is the ocean  in such bad shape  I love the analogy of a public bathroom  same reason public bathrooms are in such  bad shape nobody owns them nobody has  the incentive to to keep them up and the  users don't have the incentive to keep  them clean the public bathroom is a  common area owned by no one but hotel  bathrooms are clean because someone owns  them in fisheries it's really the same  thing if no one owns it it becomes  mismanaged scientists call it the  tragedy of the Commons think of the  ocean full of fish and we're all out  there trying to exploit it the tragedy  is that we're all going to race to try  to get a bigger share of that resource  you know that then the next guy how can  we fix this tragedy give fisherman an  incentive to make fish populations grow  it's the only thing that seems to work  according to a worldwide study conducted  by Costello and his colleagues so  instead of just having a what we call a  race to fish what they do is they  allocate the total catch among all the  fishermen it's called catch shares  because fishermen get a share of the  total catch let's say 200 tons of fish  is the total catch allowed by government  officials fishermen bob is allowed to  catch say 1% of the total catch  year after year